## Check the forecast

The **Met Office** publishes a daily **pollen forecast**. On high pollen days, try to limit time outdoors, especially in the early morning and evening.

## Keep pollen away

- put **petroleum jelly** or an allergen barrier balm around your nostrils
- wear **wraparound sunglasses**
- shower and change your clothes after being outside
- keep windows and doors shut when you can
- dry washing indoors on high pollen days
- vacuum regularly and dust with a damp cloth
- keep car windows shut and use a pollen filter if your car has one

## Driving

Some hay fever medicines, and hay fever itself, can make you drowsy. It is an offence to drive if your driving is impaired. Choose non-drowsy medicines and do not drive if you feel sleepy.

## Exams

Hay fever season falls during exam season. Students should start treatment early and tell their school or college if symptoms are bad.

## Support

**Allergy UK** runs a free helpline.
